# Complexity Comparison

| Approach       | Time     | Space    |
| -------------- | -------- | -------- |
| Brute Force    | O(n²)    | O(1)     |
| Sliding Window | **O(n)** | **O(1)** |

---

# Edge Cases

* Single element array.
* Every element is within `[l, r]`.
* No valid subarray.
* `l = 1`.
* Large array (`n = 10^5`).
* Works because **all array elements are positive**. If negative numbers were allowed, this sliding window approach would not be correct.

---

# Interview Pattern

This is a classic application of the pattern:

> **Count subarrays in a range = Count(≤ upper bound) − Count(≤ lower bound − 1)**

You'll see the same idea in problems involving subarray sums, prefix constraints, and "at most K" versus "exactly K" counts.
